 #### Breaking changes
 
+- **A note hash's nonce index lies in a fixed section chosen by where the note hash came from.** Each section is `MAX_NOTE_HASHES_PER_TX` (64) wide: private non-revertible note hashes use their index `i` in the private kernel's output, public non-revertible ones (setup) `64 + p`, private revertible ones `128 + i`, and public revertible ones (app logic, teardown) `192 + p`, where `p` is the note hash's index in the transaction's note hashes. All of them use `DOM_SEP__NOTE_HASH_NONCE`. A note created in private now has the same final note hash, and nullifier, whether or not its tx makes public calls, and its creator knows both before sending the tx. Private-only txs keep their dense indices. Nonce discovery tries all 256 indices, so contracts that rely on aztec-nr's discovery need no change. Code that recomputes a nonce itself must use the section index, such as `192 + p` for a partial note completed in app logic. A contract built against an earlier aztec-nr does not discover notes whose nonce index lies outside the first section.
 - **`compute_secret_hash` is renamed `compute_private_content_hash`, and it returns a different hash** ([#689](https://github.com/aztec-labs-eng/aztec-node/pull/689)). The two application fields of an L1-to-L2 message are renamed for what they are: `content` is the message's public content hash, and its secret hash is its private content hash. The preimage a contract reveals to consume the message is its private content, which the application defines: a single claim secret, as in the fee juice and token bridges, or something richer. The hash now uses the `DOM_SEP__PRIVATE_CONTENT_HASH` domain separator, so a contract built with this version cannot consume a message whose hash was computed the old way: claim pending messages before upgrading, or send them again afterwards. `consume_l1_to_l2_message`, `compute_l1_to_l2_message_hash` and `compute_l1_to_l2_message_nullifier` keep their arguments in the same order, under the new names.
 
